      "<figure class=\"madas-excerpt\"><img src=\"resources/madas/alevel-pure-c/q11-solution-2.svg\" alt=\"(b) Since −1 ≤cos(x −60◦) ≤1, the maximum is 2 , [M1] [A1] when cos(x −60◦) = 1. The first solution has x −60◦= 0, so it is first attained at a positive angle at 60◦. [A1] (c) The temperature is 32 + 2 cos((15t −60)◦). [B1] The maximum is 34◦C [A1] when 15t −60 = 0 in this day, at 04:00 . [A1] √ (d) For T = 30.5, 30.5 = 32 + 3 sin(15t◦) + cos(15t◦), so −1.5 = 2 cos((15t −60)◦). Set cos((15t −60)◦) = −3/4. [M1] In the allowed interval −60 ≤15t −60 &lt; 300, [A1] the two angles in degrees are arccos(−3/4) ≈138.5904 and 360 −arccos(−3/4) ≈221.4096. [A1]\" style=\"--madas-width:677.3px\"></figure>",
      "<figure class=\"madas-excerpt\"><img src=\"resources/madas/alevel-pure-c/q11-solution-3.svg\" alt=\"The angle families are 138.5904◦+ 360◦n and 221.4096◦+ 360◦n. Adding 60 and dividing by 15 gives t = 13.23936 + 24n or 18.76064 + 24n, n ∈Z. Within the day n = 0. The fractional hours give 0.23936(60) = 14.36 and 0.76064(60) = 45.64 minutes: Therefore t ≈13.23936 and 18.76064 hours, which round to 13:14 [M1] [A1] and 18:46 to the nearest minute: [A1]\" style=\"--madas-width:650.7px\"></figure>",
